Let $K\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot, meaning one oriented component of a finite smooth oriented link in the standard oriented three-sphere $S^3=\mathbb R^3\cup\{\infty\}$. The grid orientation below fixes the sign convention for its Alexander grading.

For an integer $n\ge2$, put $T_n=\mathbb R^2/(n\mathbb Z)^2$, with horizontal circles $\alpha_i=\{y=i\}$ and vertical circles $\beta_j=\{x=j\}$ for $i,j\in\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$. A **toroidal grid diagram of grid number $n$** is a pair of disjoint sets $\mathbb O,\mathbb X$ of points in the open cells of this grid such that every row and every column contains exactly one point of each set. At each crossing of the projected link, the vertical segment is over and the horizontal segment is under. Orient each horizontal segment from its $O$-marking to its $X$-marking and each vertical segment from its $X$-marking to its $O$-marking. The markings and these segments determine an oriented link. Following each component from an $O$ along its row to an $X$ and then along that column to the next $O$ defines a permutation of the $n$ $O$-markings; its cycles are the link components. The grid represents a knot precisely when this permutation is one $n$-cycle.

A **grid state** is a subset $\mathbf x\subset\bigcup_{i,j}(\alpha_i\cap\beta_j)$ containing exactly one intersection on each horizontal and each vertical circle. After numbering the circles, grid states correspond bijectively to permutations of $\{1,\ldots,n\}$, so there are $n!$ states. For states $\mathbf x,\mathbf y$, a **rectangle from $\mathbf x$ to $\mathbf y$** is an embedded torus rectangle whose sides lie in grid circles, whose northeast and southwest corners lie in $\mathbf x$, whose northwest and southeast corners lie in $\mathbf y$, and whose remaining state points agree. Rectangles are subsets of the torus, so their lifts may cross a chosen fundamental-domain edge. Such a rectangle is **empty** when its interior contains no point of $\mathbf x$; write $\operatorname{Rect}^{\circ}(\mathbf x,\mathbf y)$ for the empty rectangles from $\mathbf x$ to $\mathbf y$.

To define the gradings, fix the fundamental square $[0,n)\times[0,n)$. For finite signed point sets $P,Q$ in this square, set $I(P,Q)=\#\{(p,q):p_1<q_1,\ p_2<q_2\}$ and $J(P,Q)=(I(P,Q)+I(Q,P))/2$, extended bilinearly to signed point sets. Regard $\mathbf x$, $\mathbb O$, and $\mathbb X$ as signed point sets and define

$$M_{\mathbb O}(\mathbf x)=J(\mathbf x-\mathbb O,\mathbf x-\mathbb O)+1,\qquad M_{\mathbb X}(\mathbf x)=J(\mathbf x-\mathbb X,\mathbf x-\mathbb X)+1.$$

For a knot grid, define the rational Alexander value

$$A_G(\mathbf x)=\frac{M_{\mathbb O}(\mathbf x)-M_{\mathbb X}(\mathbf x)}{2}-\frac{n-1}{2}.$$

The lemma establishing integrality and the rectangle-change formula is a well-definedness supplier for this grading. For any rectangle $r$, let $O_i(r)$ and $X_i(r)$ be $1$ if the interior of $r$ contains the corresponding marking and $0$ otherwise; let $n_{\mathbb O}(r)=\sum_iO_i(r)$ and $n_{\mathbb X}(r)=\sum_iX_i(r)$.

For illustration, this $3\times3$ marking pattern has one marking of each type in every row and column; its wraparound arrows are interpreted on the torus.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
  \draw[step=1,gray] (0,0) grid (3,3);
  \node at (0.5,0.5) {$O$}; \node at (1.5,0.5) {$X$};
  \node at (1.5,1.5) {$O$}; \node at (2.5,1.5) {$X$};
  \node at (2.5,2.5) {$O$}; \node at (0.5,2.5) {$X$};
  \node[left] at (0,0.5) {$1$}; \node[left] at (0,1.5) {$2$}; \node[left] at (0,2.5) {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

Let $Y_0,Y_1$ be closed smooth oriented three-manifolds, let $W$ be a compact smooth oriented four-manifold with boundary $-Y_0\sqcup Y_1$, where $-Y_0$ has the reversed orientation, and let $K_i\subset Y_i$ be images of smooth oriented embeddings of the circle. Every component of $Y_i$ is required to meet $K_i$; the boundary parametrizations and compatible product collars are part of the data. A **decorated knot cobordism** from $(Y_0,K_0)$ to $(Y_1,K_1)$ is a properly embedded smooth oriented surface $\Sigma\subset W$ with boundary $(-K_0)\sqcup K_1$, together with one $w$-basepoint and one $z$-basepoint on each end knot and a compact properly embedded one-manifold $\mathcal A\subset\Sigma$, which may have arc and closed-circle components. Every component of $W$ meets $\Sigma$, and every component of $\Sigma$ meets $\mathcal A$. The full dividing set $\mathcal A$ decomposes $\Sigma$ into subsurfaces $\Sigma_w,\Sigma_z$, with each $w$-basepoint in the closure of $\Sigma_w$, each $z$-basepoint in the closure of $\Sigma_z$, and $\mathcal A$ meeting the boundary transversely away from the marked points. Every component of $K_i\setminus\mathcal A$ contains exactly one of the two marked points; in particular each boundary knot meets the dividing set in two points, separating its $w$- and $z$-intervals. The basepoint labels, dividing set, two regions and their two-color assignment are part of the data.

For an end pair $(Y,K)$, a doubly pointed Heegaard diagram consists of a closed genus-$g$ oriented surface $S$, two $g$-tuples $\boldsymbol\alpha,\boldsymbol\beta$ of pairwise disjoint simple closed curves giving a Heegaard splitting of $Y$, and points $w,z$ in the complement of those curves such that arcs in the two handlebodies from $w$ to $z$ form $K$. Each intersection point $\mathbf x\in\mathbb T_{\boldsymbol\alpha}\cap\mathbb T_{\boldsymbol\beta}$ determines a Spin-c structure $\mathfrak s_w(\mathbf x)$ on $Y$ from the homology class of the nonvanishing gradient vector field specified by $\mathbf x$ and $w$. For $\mathfrak t\in\operatorname{Spin}^c(Y)$, let $CFL^-_{UV}(Y,K,\mathfrak t)$ be generated over $R=\mathbb F_2[U,V]$ by those intersection points with $\mathfrak s_w(\mathbf x)=\mathfrak t$. A periodic domain is an integral two-chain in the regions of $S\setminus(\boldsymbol\alpha\cup\boldsymbol\beta)$ whose boundary is an integral sum of the attaching curves; its capped homology class in $Y$ is denoted $[P]$, and $n_w(P)$ is its multiplicity at $w$. Restrict to diagrams strongly admissible for $\mathfrak t$: there is a generator $\mathbf x$ with $\mathfrak s_w(\mathbf x)=\mathfrak t$, and for every nonzero periodic domain $P$ with $n_w(P)=0$ and $\langle c_1(\mathfrak t),[P]\rangle=2N>0$, some region of $P$ has multiplicity at least $N$. Here $c_1(\mathfrak t)$ is the first Chern class of its determinant line bundle. The symmetric product $\operatorname{Sym}^g(S)$ is the quotient of $S^g$ by permutations of the factors. The associated product tori $\mathbb T_{\boldsymbol\alpha},\mathbb T_{\boldsymbol\beta}\subset\operatorname{Sym}^g(S)$ determine the differential on each Spin-c summand by
$$
\partial \mathbf x=\sum_{\mathbf y}\ \sum_{\substack{\phi\in\pi_2(\mathbf x,\mathbf y)\\ \mu(\phi)=1}} \#_2\bigl(\mathcal M(\phi)/\mathbb R\bigr)\,U^{n_w(\phi)}V^{n_z(\phi)}\mathbf y.
$$
Here $\pi_2(\mathbf x,\mathbf y)$ is the set of homotopy classes of Whitney disks, $\mu$ is the Maslov index, $\mathcal M(\phi)/\mathbb R$ is the moduli space of index-one disks modulo translation for chosen generic almost-complex data, $n_w,n_z$ are their local multiplicities at the marked points, and $\#_2$ is the finite count modulo two. This is the two-variable specialization of the colored link Floer complex. Its curvature vanishes: for a knot with one $w$- and one $z$-basepoint the curvature formula is $UV+VU=0$ in $\mathbb F_2[U,V]$, so $\partial^2=0$. The complex has the two-coordinate filtration induced by the $w$- and $z$-multiplicities.

For a chosen $\mathfrak s\in\operatorname{Spin}^c(W)$, the Floer map indexed by $\mathfrak s$ has the complexes for $(Y_0,K_0,\mathfrak s|_{Y_0})$ and $(Y_1,K_1,\mathfrak s|_{Y_1})$ as source and target; $\operatorname{Spin}^c(W)$ denotes the set of Spin-c structures on $W$. The following local schematic shows the two regions separated by dividing arcs; it records decoration data and does not represent the cobordism map.

